1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,138
Lesson 35: what's new in 1.7, the new HF calculators

2
00:00:05,565 --> 00:00:20,277
YA-NEC course, lesson 35: the new HF calculators of 1.7. Calculators, HF, HF Yagi 2-3 elements.

3
00:00:20,327 --> 00:00:31,144
The starting dimensions come from I4MFA's articles: driven element, reflector 4 % longer, director 6 % shorter, and the percentage trims for tuning.

4
00:00:31,194 --> 00:00:40,529
«Tuning guide»: NEC lengthens each element by 1 % and shows what happens to resonance and F/B. It is what you do on the roof, but beforehand.

5
00:00:40,579 --> 00:00:52,863
Resonance is moved mainly by the driven element; reflector and director change the front to back. The SWR on 50 ohm stays high: you need a match, such as the gamma match.

6
00:00:52,913 --> 00:01:00,553
Interlaced dual-band Yagi: two bands on the same boom, with one feed line or two, after W1JR's idea.

7
00:01:00,603 --> 00:01:11,639
The table gives position and length of each element. The warnings check pairs of nearby elements in a 1/2, 2/3 or 7/8 ratio, which interact.

8
00:01:11,689 --> 00:01:21,676
Verticals: the new «trap multiband» type, from 40 to 10 metres, with LC traps and radials.

9
00:01:21,726 --> 00:01:28,202
«Tune the bands» uses NEC section by section, from the bottom, to find the length of each piece for each band.

10
00:01:28,252 --> 00:01:36,772
For each band: section, top trap, impedance, SWR and efficiency. Trap losses matter most on 40 metres.

11
00:01:36,822 --> 00:01:51,691
«Inverted L / T» for 160 metres: vertical section, horizontal section or T top hat, over the radials.

12
00:01:51,741 --> 00:02:15,190
«Half-sloper on the tower»: the wire slopes down and the Yagi on top acts as a top hat. «Find the lowest SWR» tries the lengths with NEC; here the wire would touch the ground, and the warning says to raise the feed point or reduce the slope.

13
00:02:15,240 --> 00:02:42,632
Dipoles: «broadband» with the coaxial resonator, a stub in parallel and a quarter wave transformer that widen the band.

14
00:02:42,682 --> 00:02:57,562
«Relay multiband»: full length sections joined by remote controlled relays, as in the article by IK4PKK and IK4CIE.

15
00:02:57,612 --> 00:03:11,364
Linear loading: it shortens the antenna with a folded line section instead of a coil.

16
00:03:11,414 --> 00:03:22,857
«Bring to resonance and compare» compares with NEC, at the same length: linear loading, a coil at the same place, a coil at the base and a top hat. Here the top hat wins.

17
00:03:22,907 --> 00:04:02,985
Wire antennas: «harmonic resonances», the EFLW wire that resonates on several bands at once.

18
00:04:03,035 --> 00:04:12,283
With the starting length the harmonics fall outside the bands. «Length for the chosen bands» looks for the wire that brings them all closest to the band centres.

19
00:04:12,333 --> 00:04:21,749
The table shows the updated resonances; the shortening factor must still be measured on site, as the RadioKit article explains.

20
00:04:21,799 --> 00:04:29,548
End of lesson 35. Next one: matching, loops, stacks and tips. 73 de IC8TEM.
